findings.body:
**Every git failure in the shape check is read as "no match"**

The per-shape `try` wraps `rev-parse --verify --quiet` and `merge-tree --write-tree --no-messages`. The two outcomes it means to swallow both exit with status 1:
- "no commit that far down", from `rev-parse --verify --quiet`;
- a conflict, from `merge-tree`.

But the catch only calls `rethrowIfKilled`, which rethrows only SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP. Every other failure is swallowed too:
- a usage exit from `merge-tree` on a git without `--write-tree` (before 2.38, e.g. Ubuntu 22.04's 2.34);
- a 128 from the plain `rev-parse --verify <mergeCommit>~N^{tree}`;
- a missing object;
- a crash.

```ts
        } catch (error) {
          // no commit that far down, or a conflict
          rethrowIfKilled(error);
        }
```

**What goes wrong.** When every shape fails this way, `mergedOnto` returns `undefined` and `pup land` refuses with "…the merge landed content the gate never measured." That message blames GitHub's merge for unmeasured content when git itself failed. The outer `Reading what ${mergeCommit} landed failed` path (L392-396), added for exactly this case, is never reached.

**Old git can reach this.** Nothing enforces a minimum git version. The gate only calls `merge-tree --write-tree` when the target moved under an open PR, so a pin can exist without it ever running. On old git, every squash or rebase land, and now every merge-commit land too, would refuse permanently with a tampering-shaped reason.

**Fix: match the gate.** The gate's own `merge-tree` caller already makes this distinction (decision 105, `src/core/merge-gate.service.ts` L1843-1846): status 1 is a conflict, and anything else is git failing to merge and is rethrown.

**Missing test.** The new git-failure test only fails `rev-list`, which runs outside this `try`. A test where `merge-tree` throws with status 128 should expect the "Reading what … landed failed" refusal.

https://github.com/AlexisBalayre/pupitre/blob/cfdf9b17a35a8b153742fc6bb108ec86c5934505/src/core/land.service.ts#L385-L388

findings.suggestion:
} catch (error) {
  // 1 is no commit that far down, or a conflict; any other status is git
  // failing outright (no `--write-tree` before 2.38), refused as such
  rethrowIfKilled(error);
  if ((error as { status?: unknown }).status !== 1) throw error;
}
